Visiting the châteaux of the Loire Valley means travelling through five centuries of French history over a distance of just a few dozen kilometres. The problem isn’t finding a château to visit, but choosing which ones and in what order. From the Château de la Huberdière in Nazelles-Négron, near Amboise, you’re right in the centre of the map. Here’s how we recommend organising your visits.

## Which Loire Valley châteaux should you visit first?

If you were to choose just four or five, start with those that best encapsulate the Renaissance. Chenonceau for its gallery overlooking the Cher, Chambord for its grandeur and its double-helix staircase, Villandry for its gardens, and Amboise and the Clos Lucé for Leonardo da Vinci.

The classic mistake is to try to do too much. Two châteaux a day is already quite a lot if you want to enjoy the gardens and the surrounding villages. It’s better to take it easy and leave some time for the Loire itself, its banks, its markets and its wine cellars.

## Must-see attractions around Amboise

Amboise is an excellent base, and it’s ours too. The royal château overlooks the Loire right in the town centre, and the Clos Lucé, just a short walk away, is home to Leonardo da Vinci’s final resting place and models of his inventions. A morning at the royal château and an afternoon at the Clos Lucé make for an ideal first day, without ever having to drive for long.

Just twenty minutes away, Chenonceau spans the River Cher with its arches and Diane de Poitiers’ garden. It is undoubtedly the most photogenic château in the valley; it’s best to visit early in the morning to avoid the crowds. Make sure you allow plenty of time for the gardens and the flower-filled kitchen garden, which are well worth a visit in their own right.

## Chambord, Chaumont, Villandry

If you’re willing to travel a little further, there are three places well worth a visit. Chambord, about an hour away, is impressive for its size and its hunting grounds: you can hire a bike or a rowing boat there to explore the park. Chaumont-sur-Loire charms visitors with its international garden festival, which takes place every year from April to November. Villandry is the epitome of French formal gardens and is best visited in spring or early summer, when the ornamental vegetable garden is at its best.

Some distance guide points from Amboise:

- Chenonceau: 20 minutes
- Chaumont-sur-Loire: 25 minutes
- Villandry: 45 minutes
- Chambord: 1 hour
- Blois: 40 minutes
- Cheverny: 50 minutes

## A three-day itinerary starting from Amboise

If it’s your first visit, here’s a tried-and-tested itinerary that gives you a chance to relax.

- Day 1: Amboise and the Clos Lucé in the morning and afternoon, followed by the market and the banks of the Loire
- Day 2: Chenonceau early in the morning, lunch in Montrichard, then Chaumont-sur-Loire and its gardens
- Day 3: Villandry and its gardens, or Chambord for a touch of extravagance, whichever you prefer

This itinerary avoids long journeys and leaves half a day free to relax. You can extend it slightly and add a fourth day to visit Blois and Cheverny, or for a wine tasting in Vouvray.

## Beyond the châteaux: vineyards and the banks of the Loire

The valley is about more than just its stone buildings. The vineyards of Vouvray and Montlouis-sur-Loire, just a few minutes from the château, offer tours of their cellars, which are often hewn out of tuffeau stone. The ‘Loire à Vélo’ cycle route runs alongside the river for kilometres along gentle cycle paths, accessible to all. The cave-dwelling villages, markets and summer open-air cafés round off a holiday that would not be complete with just the monuments.

Taking the time to enjoy these little extras transforms a tour of the châteaux into a proper holiday in Touraine. It’s often what visitors remember most.

## Practical tips for your visits

A few tips can make your visit run more smoothly. The major châteaux open early: arriving at opening time, around nine or ten o’clock, means you can enjoy the rooms and gardens before the tour groups arrive. Tickets for Chenonceau and Chambord should be booked online in advance during the high season, which avoids the queues. Many sites offer a combined ticket for the château and gardens, which is better value than two separate tickets.

Don’t forget to check opening times: some monuments close earlier out of season, and the gardens at Villandry are best visited during the day. To make your days more relaxed, alternate between a grand château in the morning and a more laid-back spot in the afternoon – perhaps a village, a wine cellar or a bike ride. You’ll get the most out of Touraine at this pace rather than rushing from one monument to the next.

## Where to stay when visiting the châteaux of the Loire Valley

The best base is a central, peaceful location from which you can explore the surrounding area without wasting time travelling. This is exactly where the Château de la Huberdière is situated: ten minutes from Amboise and right in the heart of the valley.

Staying in a château rather than a hotel makes the experience last longer: you arrive in the evening at a walled estate, have dinner at the table d’hôtes, and set off the next day feeling refreshed. We provide details of the rooms and how to organise your stay on the [stay] page (/sejour), and our guide to [bed and breakfasts in Amboise] (/blog/chambres-hotes-amboise-chateau) complements this.

## Planning your stay

A stay of three or four nights is enough to see the main sights without rushing. You can combine visits to the grand châteaux, the Vouvray vineyards, a cycle ride along the banks of the Loire and the village markets. We know the region well and love to share our itineraries, tailored to the season and your own pace.

To plan your visit and check availability, please visit the [stay](/en/sejour) page or get in touch with us. We’ll help you put together an itinerary that’s just right for you.
